🔹 Idempotent API Design – GS-Level Explanation

Definition:
An idempotent API is an API endpoint where multiple identical requests produce the same result as a single request, without causing unintended side effects.

Key idea: The API can be called once or many times, but the state of the server remains consistent.

Importance: Prevents errors in distributed systems caused by network retries, duplicate requests, or client errors.

🔹 Why Idempotent APIs are Important

Network Reliability

In microservices, network failures or timeouts may cause clients to retry requests.

Idempotency ensures retries don’t corrupt data.

Financial Transactions

Duplicate payment requests must not deduct funds twice.

Ensures correctness in banking and e-commerce operations.

Distributed Systems

Multiple services communicating over unreliable networks need predictable state changes.

🔹 HTTP Methods and Idempotency
HTTP Method	Idempotent?	Explanation
GET	✅ Yes	Fetching data doesn’t change server state.
PUT	✅ Yes	Updates resource to a fixed state. Multiple identical updates produce same result.
DELETE	✅ Yes	Deleting the same resource multiple times has the same effect as deleting once.
POST	❌ Usually	Creates new resources. Multiple identical requests may create duplicates.

Note: POST can be made idempotent using idempotency keys.

🔹 How to Make APIs Idempotent

Use Idempotency Keys

Client generates a unique request ID.

Server stores the ID and result.

Duplicate requests with the same key return the previous response.

Avoid Side Effects on Retries

Operations should either be stateless or repeatable safely.

Use PUT for Updates Instead of POST

PUT replaces resource state entirely, making retries safe.

Return Consistent Responses

Ensures client sees the same result for repeated requests.

🔹 Banking Example

Scenario: Fund transfer from Account A → Account B

Without Idempotency: Retry may debit Account A twice → incorrect balances.

With Idempotency Key:

Each transfer request has a unique ID.

If retry occurs, the system checks the ID → prevents duplicate transfer.

Outcome: only one debit/credit occurs, even if client retries.

🔹 Benefits

Data Consistency: Prevents duplicate operations.

System Reliability: Safe retries in case of network failures.

Predictable Behavior: Clients can repeat requests without fear of unintended effects.

Critical for Financial & E-Commerce Systems: Guarantees transactional correctness.

